<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sbgn xmlns="http://sbgn.org/libsbgn/0.3" xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">
  <map id="QBM-03" language="process description">
    <extension>
      <qbo:mapNotes xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">Cryptochrome radical-pair photochemistry. Cryptochrome is the only plant entity in the review's bibliography where a magnetic field was applied and an effect measured on the protein's own chemistry rather than on a downstream phenotype, so it carries the atlas's only T1 annotations. The hyperfine partners that make this pair field-sensitive are the flavin's own nitrogens and protons — 14N at 99.6% natural abundance and 1H at essentially 100% — so unlike the 57Fe route argued for iron-sulfur clusters, this one is not abundance-limited. That asymmetry is much of why cryptochrome is the strongest case here and the Fe/S nodes elsewhere are not. Phytochrome B is drawn deliberately as context with no quantum claim: its photochemistry is a Z/E isomerisation offering no spin-based route, so reported field effects on phytochrome signalling are most simply a downstream consequence of cryptochrome. Note also that one blinded replication (Harris et al. 2009) did NOT reproduce cryptochrome-dependent field effects; that result is recorded in the ontology on the same footing as the positive reports. Derived from: Figure 3 panel A of Porterfield &amp; Barker, "Plant Responses to Near-Null Magnetic Fields" — expanded to the photocycle and bound to Arabidopsis loci</qbo:mapNotes>
    </extension>
    <glyph id="nucleus" class="compartment">
      <label text="Nucleus" />
      <bbox x="-26.00" y="-26.00" w="648.51" h="301.52" />
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="organism" class="compartment">
      <label text="Organism" />
      <bbox x="179.17" y="313.52" w="238.17" h="86.64" />
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CRY1" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Cryptochrome 1" />
      <bbox x="76.99" y="0.00" w="126.95" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CRY1__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FAD" />
        <bbox x="82.99" y="-9.00" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="CRY1__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="Trp triad" />
        <bbox x="82.99" y="-9.00" w="63.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CRY1" evidenceTier="T1" confidence="verified">
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G08920</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005634</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Ahmad2007" doi="10.1007/s00425-006-0383-0" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Magnetic intensity affects cryptochrome-dependent responses in Arabidopsis thaliana.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Solovyov2007" doi="10.1529/biophysj.106.097139" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Theoretical analysis of Arabidopsis cryptochrome-1 shows magnetic fields could alter activation yields through radical-pair spin dynamics.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Harris2009" doi="10.1098/rsif.2008.0519" direction="no_effect" fieldRegime="static_MF">Under blinded conditions, cryptochrome-dependent magnetic-field effects in Arabidopsis were NOT reproduced.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Pooam2019" doi="10.1007/s00425-018-3002-y" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Magnetic sensitivity mediated by Arabidopsis cryptochrome occurs during flavin reoxidation in the dark, not during photoreduction.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Xu2014" doi="10.1016/j.asr.2014.01.033" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Blue-light-dependent phosphorylation of cryptochromes is affected by magnetic fields in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>The reference case for the radical-pair mechanism in biology, and the entity from which the review's whole argument is generalised outward.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Direction of effect is not consistent across the literature, and one blinded replication attempt failed (see Harris2009 below). Treat the magnitude as unsettled even though the mechanism is well characterised.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CRY2" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Cryptochrome 2" />
      <bbox x="237.94" y="0.00" w="126.84" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CRY2__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FAD" />
        <bbox x="243.94" y="-9.00" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="CRY2__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="flowering" />
        <bbox x="243.94" y="-9.00" w="63.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CRY2" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G04400</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005634</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Xu2012" doi="10.1016/j.asr.2011.12.004" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">A near-null magnetic field affects cryptochrome-related hypocotyl growth and flowering in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Agliassa2018b" doi="10.1016/j.jphotobiol.2018.05.027" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Geomagnetic field variation impacts cryptochrome and phytochrome signalling.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>Same FAD/tryptophan-triad chemistry as CRY1. CRY2's route to flowering time is the mechanistic link between the photoreceptor evidence and the most reproducible NNMF phenotype in the literature — delayed flowering.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="PHYB" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Phytochrome B" />
      <bbox x="398.78" y="0.00" w="120.73" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="PHYB__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="no spin route" />
        <bbox x="404.78" y="-9.00" w="91.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:PHYB" evidenceTier="T4" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G18790</qbo:xref>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CRY_FAD" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="FAD (FADox / FAD·− / FADH·)" />
      <bbox x="0.00" y="113.84" w="206.12" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="CRY_FAD__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="14N 99.6%" />
        <bbox x="6.00" y="104.84" w="63.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="CRY_FAD__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="1H ~100%" />
        <bbox x="6.00" y="104.84" w="56.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CRY_FAD" evidenceTier="T1" confidence="verified">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:57692</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Pooam2019" doi="10.1007/s00425-018-3002-y" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">The magnetically sensitive step is flavin reoxidation in the dark rather than photoreduction, implying a longer-lived radical pair than first assumed.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>The hyperfine partners that make this radical pair field-sensitive are the isoalloxazine ring's own nitrogens and protons — 14N at 99.6% abundance and 1H at essentially 100%. Unlike the 57Fe route argued for Fe/S clusters, this one is not abundance-limited, which is a large part of why cryptochrome is the strongest case in the atlas.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="TRP_TRIAD" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Tryptophan triad" />
      <bbox x="240.12" y="107.44" w="148.99" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="TRP_TRIAD__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="Trp·+ donor" />
        <bbox x="246.12" y="98.44" w="77.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:TRP_TRIAD" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_donor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>hyperfine_coupled</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G08920</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G04400</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Solovyov2007" doi="10.1529/biophysj.106.097139" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Spin dynamics of the FAD-tryptophan radical pair in Arabidopsis cryptochrome-1 can be modulated by an applied magnetic field.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>The donor half of the canonical pair. Its indole nitrogen and ring protons supply the hyperfine coupling on the donor side.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="SINGLET_TRIPLET" class="process" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Singlet ⇄ triplet mixing" />
      <bbox x="423.11" y="113.84" w="173.40" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="SINGLET_TRIPLET__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="needs ~us lifetime" />
        <bbox x="429.11" y="104.84" w="126.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:SINGLET_TRIPLET" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>spin_correlated_pair</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>hyperfine_coupled</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Ritz2000" doi="10.1016/S0006-3495(00)76629-X" direction="altered" fieldRegime="GMF">A photoreceptor-based radical-pair model can in principle transduce a geomagnetic-strength field into a change in signalling yield.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="ZadehHaghighi2022" doi="10.1098/rsif.2022.0325" direction="altered" fieldRegime="GMF">Review of magnetic field effects in biology from the radical-pair perspective, covering the lifetime and hyperfine conditions required.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>This is the mechanism itself rather than a molecule. It is on the map because the review's argument is that this step, not the protein, is what generalises to other flavoproteins and Fe/S centres.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>For a field of geomagnetic magnitude to compete, the radical pair must live long enough for mixing to accumulate — roughly the microsecond scale. That lifetime requirement is what most candidate sites elsewhere in this atlas have not been shown to meet.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COP1_SPA" class="complex" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="COP1 / SPA complex" />
      <bbox x="221.11" y="214.88" w="154.29" h="34.64" />
